1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:03,301
You typed one word into the triage chat: hello.

2
00:00:03,467 --> 00:00:09,099
And three things came back. A greeting for you.
A summary written for a parent in China.

3
00:00:09,099 --> 00:00:12,619
And, folded away, a receipt:
thought for three seconds,

4
00:00:12,619 --> 00:00:16,907
sources, a check that passed,
a judgment, and a model with a price.

5
00:00:17,067 --> 00:00:22,486
But the app is built on the OpenAI API,
and an API call returns text.

6
00:00:22,806 --> 00:00:24,832
Nobody asked for a parent summary.

7
00:00:25,152 --> 00:00:27,115
So who decided there would be one?

8
00:00:27,435 --> 00:00:29,235
And who checked it?

9
00:00:32,400 --> 00:00:34,600
Here is the picture most of us carry:

10
00:00:34,600 --> 00:00:38,821
the page sends your words to the model,
and the model sends words back.

11
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:43,740
Here is what really happens.
The page talks only to our own small server,

12
00:00:43,740 --> 00:00:47,896
and the server talks to the model.
The API key lives only there.

13
00:00:48,067 --> 00:00:51,203
The code wrapped around a model call
is called a harness.

14
00:00:51,523 --> 00:00:55,757
Ours does four jobs: build the request,
set the shape of the reply,

15
00:00:55,757 --> 00:00:59,928
check the reply, and, back in the page,
turn it into what you saw.

16
00:01:03,667 --> 00:01:05,302
Start with the request.

17
00:01:05,352 --> 00:01:07,422
Your hello is the smallest part of it.

18
00:01:07,600 --> 00:01:12,330
On top sit 15 official pages
from the university's health service

19
00:01:12,330 --> 00:01:16,041
and its student insurance,
cleaned into plain text:

20
00:01:16,041 --> 00:01:20,699
about 25,000 tokens,
the word pieces a model reads and bills by.

21
00:01:21,019 --> 00:01:22,250
A small booklet.

22
00:01:22,300 --> 00:01:25,595
Below them,
89 checked facts from our fact cards:

23
00:01:25,595 --> 00:01:28,487
every phone number, price and opening hour.

24
00:01:28,807 --> 00:01:32,988
Each card quotes the sentence it came from;
the model gets just the fact.

25
00:01:33,167 --> 00:01:37,062
Then the rules, in plain language.
Never diagnose.

26
00:01:37,062 --> 00:01:39,788
Never give a dose. In an emergency,

27
00:01:39,788 --> 00:01:41,350
action before money.

28
00:01:41,350 --> 00:01:45,178
And a parent in China
cannot dial 911 for Boston.

29
00:01:45,334 --> 00:01:48,662
Last comes a description
of the form the answer must fill.

30
00:01:48,982 --> 00:01:53,846
Together, these four parts are the instructions;
chat apps call them the system prompt.

31
00:01:54,000 --> 00:01:56,328
They never change, and that matters.

32
00:01:56,328 --> 00:02:00,079
OpenAI's cache matches a request
from its very first word,

33
00:02:00,079 --> 00:02:04,218
so whatever never changes must come first.
After the first call,

34
00:02:04,218 --> 00:02:08,099
the long top is read from the cache.
This is prompt caching,

35
00:02:08,099 --> 00:02:12,368
and on the Luna model a cached token
costs a tenth of a fresh one.

36
00:02:12,534 --> 00:02:17,909
Then what changes: the chat so far,
who is asking, the time in Boston and in China,

37
00:02:17,909 --> 00:02:21,472
whether the student clinic is open,
and finally, hello.

38
00:02:21,634 --> 00:02:25,452
Your hello carried the whole booklet,
and cost four hundredths of a cent.

39
00:02:25,600 --> 00:02:28,766
Pause here.
Why does our code tell the model the time,

40
00:02:28,766 --> 00:02:33,046
and whether the clinic is open,
instead of letting the model work it out?

41
00:02:37,700 --> 00:02:39,513
Because a model has no clock.

42
00:02:39,833 --> 00:02:44,818
Code reads the posted hours and the time zone,
works it out the same way every time,

43
00:02:44,818 --> 00:02:47,727
and writes 'to be confirmed'
when it cannot know.

44
00:02:53,267 --> 00:02:55,202
Next, the shape of the reply.

45
00:02:55,202 --> 00:02:59,006
We do not ask the model for an answer.
We hand it a form.

46
00:02:59,167 --> 00:03:03,435
13 boxes, each with a name and a type.
Some take free text.

47
00:03:03,435 --> 00:03:07,703
Some take only fixed choices:
urgency is one of six levels,

48
00:03:07,703 --> 00:03:12,116
from possibly life-threatening down
to just a rules question.

49
00:03:12,267 --> 00:03:14,678
The API calls this structured outputs.

50
00:03:14,998 --> 00:03:18,053
It is still one call,
and the reply is still text,

51
00:03:18,053 --> 00:03:19,947
just text shaped like the form.

52
00:03:20,267 --> 00:03:24,576
In strict mode nothing else fits:
no missing box, no extra box.

53
00:03:24,734 --> 00:03:27,272
Now look at one box: the parent summary.

54
00:03:27,272 --> 00:03:32,411
The rules give it an instruction:
two or three sentences for the parent in China.

55
00:03:32,411 --> 00:03:37,043
What is happening, where to go,
what the parent can do. Always filled in.

56
00:03:37,200 --> 00:03:39,008
That is the whole secret:

57
00:03:39,008 --> 00:03:43,131
one call fills all 13 boxes,
the parent summary included.

58
00:03:43,451 --> 00:03:47,589
The model did not decide to be thoughtful;
the rules say fill that box.

59
00:03:47,909 --> 00:03:50,833
And strict mode only promises the box is there,

60
00:03:50,833 --> 00:03:54,566
not that anything is in it,
so the checker rejects it empty.

61
00:03:54,734 --> 00:03:58,981
The receipt works the same way.
Sources is the citations box.

62
00:03:58,981 --> 00:04:03,438
Judgment is the urgency and where-to-go boxes,
put into Chinese.

63
00:04:10,900 --> 00:04:13,396
The filled form does not go to the screen yet.

64
00:04:13,716 --> 00:04:16,834
First it passes a checker we wrote,
called the lint,

65
00:04:16,834 --> 00:04:19,412
after the tools that flag mistakes in code.

66
00:04:19,567 --> 00:04:22,448
More than twenty rules read every line.

67
00:04:22,448 --> 00:04:26,216
They stop a dose,
like two tablets every six hours.

68
00:04:26,216 --> 00:04:30,674
A diagnosis.
A phone number or price that is on no fact card.

69
00:04:30,674 --> 00:04:35,204
Money before 911 in an emergency.
And an empty parent summary.

70
00:04:35,767 --> 00:04:38,843
If anything fails,
the form goes back with a note:

71
00:04:38,843 --> 00:04:41,612
fix these problems and return the whole form.

72
00:04:41,932 --> 00:04:43,483
One rewrite, no more.

73
00:04:43,533 --> 00:04:45,787
If the second version still fails,

74
00:04:45,787 --> 00:04:49,102
or the call breaks,
nobody sees the model's words.

75
00:04:49,102 --> 00:04:52,181
A fallback we wrote ourselves goes out instead:

76
00:04:52,181 --> 00:04:56,440
call 911 now if it was urgent,
otherwise call the student clinic.

77
00:04:56,600 --> 00:05:01,805
And once any version says emergency, the rewrite
and the fallback must stay emergency.

78
00:05:02,125 --> 00:05:06,605
A rewrite should fix what the checker flagged,
not change its mind about danger.

79
00:05:06,925 --> 00:05:10,488
If we must be wrong,
we would rather be wrong toward 911.

80
00:05:10,667 --> 00:05:14,402
But the rules already told the model never
to diagnose.

81
00:05:14,402 --> 00:05:16,202
Why check again?

82
00:05:20,167 --> 00:05:23,175
Because an instruction is a request,
not a guarantee.

83
00:05:23,495 --> 00:05:27,058
The check row on your receipt says
which path the answer took:

84
00:05:27,058 --> 00:05:29,127
passed, rewritten once, or replaced.

85
00:05:31,967 --> 00:05:36,276
Only a checked form reaches the page,
and the page lays it out like a chat.

86
00:05:36,434 --> 00:05:40,100
Urgent steps first, in a red block.
Then the explanation,

87
00:05:40,100 --> 00:05:44,089
the places to go, what to bring,
a sentence to say in English,

88
00:05:44,089 --> 00:05:48,978
and near the end the folded parent summary,
with a copy button for students.

89
00:05:49,134 --> 00:05:52,747
And the receipt?
Sources and judgment come from the form.

90
00:05:52,747 --> 00:05:54,608
Check comes from the checker.

91
00:05:54,608 --> 00:05:58,200
Model and price come from the server
and its price list.

92
00:05:58,367 --> 00:06:02,472
And process? Luna is a reasoning model.
Before writing a word,

93
00:06:02,472 --> 00:06:06,246
it reasons in hidden steps
that we pay for but never see,

94
00:06:06,246 --> 00:06:09,533
and the effort setting
in the menu says how much.

95
00:06:09,533 --> 00:06:11,278
The page times each phase:

96
00:06:11,278 --> 00:06:14,721
thinking until the first word arrives,
then writing.

97
00:06:14,721 --> 00:06:18,826
So the thinking is real.
What it contains is the next episode.

98
00:06:19,000 --> 00:06:20,800
Now a question to sit with.

99
00:06:20,941 --> 00:06:23,608
Two of the five rows are the model's own words.

100
00:06:23,928 --> 00:06:25,728
The checker reads one of them.

101
00:06:25,955 --> 00:06:27,755
Which one does nothing ever check?

102
00:06:32,100 --> 00:06:37,107
Sources. The checker reads the judgment
to make sure an emergency comes first,

103
00:06:37,107 --> 00:06:41,280
and it tests every phone number
and price against the fact cards.

104
00:06:41,280 --> 00:06:46,159
But nothing reads the source list.
It is simply what the model says it used.

105
00:06:49,267 --> 00:06:51,699
This design is not where the work started.

106
00:06:52,019 --> 00:06:57,459
The pull request behind it ran 36 hours
and 60 commits, written with Claude Code.

107
00:06:57,634 --> 00:07:03,896
On Tuesday, the knowledge base came first,
and the sources turned out to disagree.

108
00:07:03,896 --> 00:07:09,380
One insurance document puts
the out-of-pocket maximum at 5,550 dollars,

109
00:07:09,380 --> 00:07:10,693
another at 5,500.

110
00:07:10,693 --> 00:07:15,810
Seven conflicts like that were ruled on,
or marked to be confirmed.

111
00:07:15,967 --> 00:07:17,806
The first engine was rules:

112
00:07:17,806 --> 00:07:21,621
code judged how urgent a symptom
was from keyword lists,

113
00:07:21,621 --> 00:07:25,055
and the model only extracted details
and explained.

114
00:07:25,375 --> 00:07:28,878
We also built an evaluation
to compare six approaches,

115
00:07:28,878 --> 00:07:31,604
then shelved it before the comparison ran.

116
00:07:31,767 --> 00:07:34,924
Then, at nine twenty-one
on Tuesday night, the turn.

117
00:07:35,244 --> 00:07:38,188
The keyword lists kept
missing real warning signs

118
00:07:38,188 --> 00:07:39,988
and flagging harmless ones.

119
00:07:40,130 --> 00:07:45,144
So you decided: let a reasoning model judge,
with the whole booklet in front of it,

120
00:07:45,144 --> 00:07:48,044
and make the output check the only safety layer.

121
00:07:48,200 --> 00:07:51,528
Twenty-two minutes later,
the plainest chat box existed.

122
00:07:51,848 --> 00:07:53,475
Forty more commits followed,

123
00:07:53,475 --> 00:07:57,544
most of them closing a way a bad sentence
could slip past the checker.

124
00:07:57,700 --> 00:08:01,664
Notice the twist:
word rules were dropped for judging symptoms,

125
00:08:01,664 --> 00:08:06,383
yet the safety net is still word rules,
now aimed at the model's sentences.

126
00:08:06,703 --> 00:08:08,687
That trade-off is episode four.

127
00:08:16,933 --> 00:08:20,338
From the outside,
this looks like ChatGPT or Claude;

128
00:08:20,338 --> 00:08:23,721
the look was borrowed
from Claude's app on purpose,

129
00:08:23,721 --> 00:08:25,521
and that is episode three.

130
00:08:25,765 --> 00:08:27,792
Inside, it is a different machine.

131
00:08:27,966 --> 00:08:30,488
A general chat app answers in free prose,

132
00:08:30,488 --> 00:08:33,747
guided by its system prompt
and the model's training.

133
00:08:34,067 --> 00:08:37,842
Ours has a system prompt too,
and adds a form we designed,

134
00:08:37,842 --> 00:08:39,688
prices only from fact cards,

135
00:08:39,688 --> 00:08:43,049
and our own rules
between the model and the screen.

136
00:08:43,600 --> 00:08:49,195
That form is also the key to what comes next,
because a new feature is mostly a new box.

137
00:08:49,195 --> 00:08:53,072
A note to forward to a roommate.
Questions to ask the doctor.

138
00:08:53,233 --> 00:08:57,723
Each new box needs three things:
a line on the form with its instruction,

139
00:08:57,723 --> 00:09:00,614
a rule in the checker, and a place on the page.

140
00:09:00,934 --> 00:09:03,558
That recipe holds whichever agent builds it.

141
00:09:08,533 --> 00:09:10,432
So: one hello, four jobs.

142
00:09:10,600 --> 00:09:14,538
Build the request,
unchanging parts first so they are cached.

143
00:09:14,538 --> 00:09:15,636
Hand over a form.

144
00:09:15,636 --> 00:09:19,054
Check every line,
allow one rewrite, keep a fallback.

145
00:09:19,054 --> 00:09:21,117
Then lay it out, with a receipt.

146
00:09:21,266 --> 00:09:25,251
Three questions to test yourself.
Which part of the request is cached,

147
00:09:25,251 --> 00:09:29,862
and why does its position matter?
Why may a rewrite never take back an emergency?

148
00:09:29,862 --> 00:09:33,106
And what would you change
to add a note for the roommate?

149
00:09:33,266 --> 00:09:37,155
Next time: what the model is doing
during thought for three seconds,

150
00:09:37,155 --> 00:09:40,242
and why we show you a receipt
instead of its thoughts.
